# Constraints for regenerating `requirements.lock`.
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# `requirements.lock` is generated by uv. A plain re-resolve floats every
# package to the newest release, which turns a one-line security bump into a
# 54-package upgrade (fastapi, starlette, sqlalchemy, langchain-core, openai,
# ...). That is a platform upgrade, not a security fix, and it must not be
# smuggled in behind a CVE reference.
#
# This file pins every package of the previously reviewed lock, so that
# regenerating the lock changes only what is intentional:
#
#     uv pip compile requirements.txt -o requirements.lock \
#         --constraint requirements.lock.constraints
#
# The pins below are the *reviewed* set: each one is a version that was
# installed, tested and audited. Do not let a plain re-resolve float them.
#
# `certifi` was moved from 2026.5.20 to 2026.10.19 to fix PYSEC-2026-4320
# (the release bundles an eSign root CA whose intermediate keys are
# compromised). Raise this pin when a new advisory appears.
#
# To adopt a newer version of a package on purpose: change its pin here, then
# regenerate the lock and re-run the audit and the test suite.
#
# The packages removed by the 2026-10-21 dependency-security change
# (streamlit, reportlab and their exclusive subtree, including pillow) are
# intentionally absent: they are no longer part of the runtime dependency set.
#
